{# Light or dark, drawn as the two things it chooses between.

   The control depicts the themes instead of wearing the current one, which is
   why nothing in it takes a palette token: the dark track stays dark on a light
   page and on a dark one, and a switch that restyled itself each time would
   stop being a picture of the choice.

   Collapsed it is a disc the size of "back to top" -- a pale face carrying a
   rayed sun or a crescent moon, with a ring of its own sky around it. The glyph
   is what makes it legible: a graded circle is a circle, and the first version
   of this asked the colour alone to say "sun", which it does not. Hovered or focused the sky stretches to the
   left, the disc slides to its side, and the half it uncovers is named in
   words: Light, or Dark.

   The word is the state you are in, matching the disc beside it, because that
   is what the references for this pattern do and because "the other one" is a
   coin toss to read without a second cue.

   Leftward because the button is pinned to the right edge, so that is the only
   direction it can grow without moving out from under the pointer.

   The two spans are scenery and are hidden from a screen reader; the whole
   meaning is the button's name and its `aria-pressed`, which is also what the
   stylesheet reads, so there is one source for "is it dark". #}
<button type="button" class="theme-toggle js-theme" aria-pressed="false">
    <span class="theme-toggle__track" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="theme-toggle__word theme-toggle__word--light" aria-hidden="true">Light</span>
    <span class="theme-toggle__word theme-toggle__word--dark" aria-hidden="true">Dark</span>
    <span class="theme-toggle__disc" aria-hidden="true">
        <i class="fas fa-sun theme-toggle__glyph theme-toggle__glyph--sun"></i>
        <i class="fas fa-moon theme-toggle__glyph theme-toggle__glyph--moon"></i>
    </span>
    <span class="visually-hidden">Dark theme</span>
</button>
